{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>The selection controls are native <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> elements with
     <code>appearance:none</code> — the CSS draws the 18px boxes, rings and
     the switch pill, the platform keeps the state machine, keyboard
     behavior and form semantics. Nothing about a form's payload changes
     when you adopt them.</p>
  <h3>Pick the right one</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Checkbox</strong> — independent yes/no options, or a select-many
        list. Checking one never implies anything about the others.</li>
    <li><strong>Radio</strong> — exactly one choice from 2–5 visible options.
        Group them with a real <code>fieldset</code>/<code>legend</code> so
        screen readers announce what the group is about.</li>
    <li><strong>Switch</strong> — one setting that takes effect immediately
        (no Save button). Style-wise it is a checkbox with
        <code>role="switch"</code>; semantically it is still a boolean input.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use them when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>More than ~5 radio options — that is a select (next in this release
        family); visible radios don't scale.</li>
    <li>The choice triggers navigation or a destructive action on toggle —
        switches commit instantly; don't point one at <em>Delete</em>.</li>
    <li>You need a tri-state (checked/unchecked/indeterminate) parent
        checkbox — indeterminate is not styled in 0.3; nest checkboxes flat
        instead.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

